# Choreo Analysis: Centrality & Domain Heatmaps

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:choreo, path: "/home/mafinar/repos/elixir/choreo"},
  {:kino_vizjs, "~> 0.8.0"}
])
```

## Section

> **Visualizing Hotspots:** This walkthrough demonstrates how to use Choreo's heatmap engine to visualize structural importance, security risk, execution latency, and data throughput.

---

## 1. Infrastructure: Structural Importance

Infrastructure diagrams use **Centrality Heatmaps** to identify critical connectors—services that, if they failed, would disconnect large parts of the system.

```elixir
alias Choreo
alias Choreo.Analysis

system =
  Choreo.new()
  |> Choreo.add_load_balancer(:lb, name: "Gateway")
  |> Choreo.add_service(:auth, name: "Auth Svc")
  |> Choreo.add_service(:api, name: "API")
  |> Choreo.add_service(:users, name: "Users")
  |> Choreo.add_service(:billing, name: "Billing")
  |> Choreo.add_service(:orders, name: "Orders")
  |> Choreo.add_service(:inventory, name: "Inventory")
  |> Choreo.add_database(:user_db, name: "User DB")
  |> Choreo.add_database(:order_db, name: "Order DB")
  |> Choreo.connect(:lb, :auth)
  |> Choreo.connect(:lb, :api)
  |> Choreo.connect(:api, :auth)
  |> Choreo.connect(:api, :users)
  |> Choreo.connect(:api, :orders)
  |> Choreo.connect(:orders, :billing)
  |> Choreo.connect(:orders, :inventory)
  |> Choreo.connect(:users, :user_db)
  |> Choreo.connect(:orders, :order_db)

# Render Structural Importance (Betweenness Centrality)
heat_system = Analysis.heatmap(system, measure: :betweenness, palette: :heat)

Kino.VizJS.render(Choreo.to_dot(heat_system), height: "500px")
```

---

## 2. Workflow: Execution Latency

Workflow diagrams use **Latency Heatmaps** to identify the "Long Poles"—tasks that contribute the most to end-to-end execution time.

```elixir
alias Choreo.Workflow
alias Choreo.Workflow.Analysis, as: WorkflowAnalysis

wf = Workflow.new()
  |> Workflow.add_start(:start)
  |> Workflow.add_task(:validate, timeout_ms: 1000)
  |> Workflow.add_task(:process, timeout_ms: 5000, retry: 3, retry_backoff_ms: 1000)
  |> Workflow.add_task(:notify, timeout_ms: 2000)
  |> Workflow.add_end(:done)
  |> Workflow.connect(:start, :validate)
  |> Workflow.connect(:validate, :process)
  |> Workflow.connect(:process, :notify)
  |> Workflow.connect(:notify, :done)

# Render Latency Heatmap
# Note: :process will be the "hottest" because 5000ms + (3 * 1000ms) = 8000ms
heat_wf = WorkflowAnalysis.heatmap(wf, palette: :spectral)

Kino.VizJS.render(Workflow.to_dot(heat_wf), height: "300px")
```

---

## 3. Dataflow: Throughput Volume

Dataflow diagrams use **Throughput Heatmaps** to identify high-volume pipes and stages where data pressure is highest.

```elixir
alias Choreo.Dataflow
alias Choreo.Dataflow.Analysis, as: DataflowAnalysis

pipeline = Dataflow.new()
  |> Dataflow.add_source(:low_stream, rate: 10)
  |> Dataflow.add_source(:high_stream, rate: 1000)
  |> Dataflow.add_merge(:join)
  |> Dataflow.add_sink(:output)
  |> Dataflow.connect(:low_stream, :join)
  |> Dataflow.connect(:high_stream, :join)
  |> Dataflow.connect(:join, :output)

# Render Throughput Heatmap
heat_df = DataflowAnalysis.heatmap(pipeline, palette: :cool)

Kino.VizJS.render(Dataflow.to_dot(heat_df), height: "300px")
```

---

## 4. ThreatModel: Security Risk

Threat models use **Risk Heatmaps** to identify security hotspots—nodes with the highest density of STRIDE threats.

```elixir
alias Choreo.ThreatModel
alias Choreo.ThreatModel.Analysis, as: ThreatAnalysis

model = ThreatModel.new()
  |> ThreatModel.add_trust_boundary("internet", level: 0)
  |> ThreatModel.add_trust_boundary("app", level: 2)
  |> ThreatModel.add_external_entity(:hacker, boundary: "internet")
  |> ThreatModel.add_process(:web, boundary: "app")
  |> ThreatModel.add_process(:logic, boundary: "app")
  |> ThreatModel.add_data_store(:db, boundary: "app")
  |> ThreatModel.data_flow(:hacker, :web)
  |> ThreatModel.data_flow(:web, :logic)
  |> ThreatModel.data_flow(:logic, :db)

# Render Risk Heatmap
# High threat density nodes (like the web entry point) will be red.
heat_tm = ThreatAnalysis.heatmap(model, palette: :heat)

Kino.VizJS.render(ThreatModel.to_dot(heat_tm), height: "500px")
```

---

## Palettes & Legends

You can use different palettes to suit your visual needs.

```elixir
# Show the predefined legends
Kino.VizJS.render(Choreo.to_dot(Analysis.legend(:heat)), height: "150px")
Kino.VizJS.render(Choreo.to_dot(Analysis.legend(:cool)), height: "150px")
Kino.VizJS.render(Choreo.to_dot(Analysis.legend(:spectral)), height: "150px")
```
